UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 8, Week 7, Lesson 3: Rhetorical devices in verse', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Rhetorical devices in verse (anaphora, rhetorical question, direct address, antithesis) persuade as well as decorate. Analysts name the device and explain how it positions an audience.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nIdentify two rhetorical devices in a verse extract and explain each audience effect in one sentence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: devices named correctly; effects audience-focused.\n\nCHECK\nListing devices with no effect, or inventing devices not present.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nIndependently annotate a spoken-word or protest poem for three rhetorical moves and write a short evaluation of which move is most persuasive and why.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"Rhetorical devices in verse (anaphora, rhetorical question, direct address, antithesis) persuade as well as decorate. Analysts name the device and explain how it positions an audience.","lesson_number":3,"lesson_role":"Language and vocabulary","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN4-URB-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Independently annotate a spoken-word or protest poem for three rhetorical moves and write a short evaluation of which move is most persuasive and why. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using rhetoric on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Listing devices with no effect, or inventing devices not present.. Correction: Strong answer: devices named correctly;. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Listing devices with no effect, or inventing devices not present. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating rhetoric on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with rhetoric, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about rhetoric with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for rhetoric. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for rhetoric. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["devices named correctly;","Listing devices with no effect, or inventing devices not present.","Ignore the words and invent a more exciting event.","Skip the prompt because the first idea sounded good."],"question":"Identify two rhetorical devices in a verse extract and explain each audience effect in one sentence. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for rhetoric; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Careful use of verse with clear evidence.","Listing devices with no effect, or inventing devices not present.","Rhetorical devices in verse (anaphora, rhetorical question, direct address, antithesis) persuade as well as decorate.","devices named correctly;"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with rhetoric?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named verse, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name verse only, without linking it to rhetoric.","Apply rhetoric to new material and name the verse used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Listing devices with no effect, or inventing devices not present."],"question":"Which response best applies rhetoric with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries rhetoric. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event.","Say the writing is good without naming any words.","Listing devices with no effect, or inventing devices not present.","Point to exact words in the sentence that show rhetoric and say what they do."],"question":"Read: \"At dawn, Mina whispered to her goat as frost glittered on the fence.\" Which choice best uses rhetoric from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill with textual evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Listing devices with no effect, or inventing devices not present.","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged.","Name verse without linking it to any words in the text.","Rhetorical devices in verse (anaphora, rhetorical question, direct address, antithesis) persuade as well as decorate."],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about rhetoric?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Lengthen the answer with adjectives while keeping the same idea.","Locate precise textual evidence, then explain how language choices shape meaning or effect.","Guess from the title alone with no textual clue.","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged."],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to analyse verse?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake.","Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess.","Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length.","Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea."],"question":"Which revision actually improves meaning for rhetoric?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Accurate transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Change fonts and claim the work is new.","Listing devices with no effect, or inventing devices not present.","Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about rhetoric.","Retype the worked model as the final answer."],"question":"Which response shows accurate use of rhetoric on fresh material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about rhetoric with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating rhetoric on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using rhetoric on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for rhetoric. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with rhetoric, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Listing devices with no effect, or inventing devices not present.. Correction: Strong answer: devices named correctly;. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s4-eng-y8-w07-l3","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise rhetoric.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with a stronger revision.","Change meaning or accuracy, not only length.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh topic that still uses the same skill.","Explain one decision in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include an original response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use rhetoric with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify verse and explain why it matters.","I can practise anaphora independently on a new prompt.","I can notice audience needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Rhetorical devices in verse (anaphora, rhetorical question, direct address, antithesis) persuade as well as decorate.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation.","Rhetorical devices in verse (anaphora, rhetorical question, direct address, antithesis) persuade as well as decorate.","A claim unrelated to the text."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about rhetoric?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nLines repeating ''We still—'' create anaphora that accumulates urgency. A rhetorical question (''Who speaks for the quiet?'') implicates listeners without demanding a literal answer in the moment.\n\nTRY FIRST\nIdentify two rhetorical devices in a verse extract and explain each audience effect in one sentence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: devices named correctly; effects audience-focused.","explain":"Rhetorical devices in verse (anaphora, rhetorical question, direct address, antithesis) persuade as well as decorate. Analysts name the device and explain how it positions an audience.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Listing devices with no effect, or inventing devices not present. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Rhetoric in verse.","caption":"Audience position.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Device</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">anaphora</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Effect</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">urgency</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Lines repeating ''We still—'' create anaphora that accumulates urgency. A rhetorical question (''Who speaks for the quiet?'' Then write your own attempt.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Lines repeating ''We still—'' create anaphora that accumulates urgency. A rhetorical question (''Who speaks for the quiet?'' Then write your own attempt."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A guess with no explanation.","Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about rhetoric?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nLines repeating ''We still—'' create anaphora that accumulates urgency. A rhetorical question (''Who speaks for the quiet?'') implicates listeners without demanding a literal answer in the moment. Think about how this word or relationship creates meaning.\n\nTRY FIRST\nUnderline or quote one precise detail in the model and explain its effect.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning.","explain":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather t